ll connected devices. By segmenting the network in this manner, switches enable more efficient data transmission and a more robust network infrastructure. The concept of a collision domain is therefore directly managed and partitioned by switch functionality. [Data: Entities (626); Relationships (2476,+more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"MAC Address Learning and Forwarding\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The process of MAC Learning is essential for populating the MAC Address Table. This table is used by switches and bridges to store MAC addresses and their corresponding port numbers, which directly influences packet forwarding decisions. When a network device sends a frame, the MAC Learning process observes the source MAC address and adds it to the table. Subsequently, during packet forwarding, the MAC Address Table guides the switch to send the packet out of the specific port associated with the destination MAC address, rather than broadcasting it to all ports. The Aging Timer mechanism is also part of this system, used to remove outdated or inactive entries from the MAC Address Table, ensuring the table remains current and accurate. [Data: Entities (1794, 1847, 1848, 1849); Relationships (2517, 2518, 2519)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Broadcast Domain Behavior with Bridges and Switches\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Unlike collision domains, broadcast domains are not segmented by bridges and switches. This means that broadcast messages sent by any device within a broadcast domain will be propagated to all other devices within that same domain. Bridges and switches, while effective at segmenting collision domains, do not perform this segmentation for broadcast traffic. Therefore, a broadcast domain encompasses a set of devices that will receive broadcast packets. Understanding this limitation is crucial for network design and management, particularly in larger networks where excessive broadcasts can degrade performance. [Data: Entities (1846, 1791);